# Preserved upstream bytes. `-text` stops any end-of-line conversion from rewriting a
# checkout and breaking the recorded digests; `-whitespace` stops `git diff --check` from
# reporting trailing whitespace that belongs to the upstream document and must stay there;
# `-diff` stops git from rendering a document as a text diff, which its content heuristic
# otherwise does for PDFs whose streams happen to look textual. It arrived with the
# certified-bounds wave on 2026-08-05, copied from the region-search source record where a
# vendored PDF rendered as 3 052 added lines in review. It is prophylactic here: no PDF
# currently under this directory triggers the heuristic, which README.md records with the
# one command that checks it. Set anyway so the next document vendored here cannot
# reintroduce the problem silently. It protects the review, never the bytes — every blob is
# checked against its manifest digest either way.
# These files are evidence, not source: the only correct edit is replacing a whole document
# with a newly retrieved edition and updating its record.
* -text -whitespace -diff

# The record itself is ordinary repository text and is checked like any other file. Each of
# the three attributes above is re-enabled by name, `diff` included: the glob's `-diff` is
# for preserved documents only, and leaving it in force here would make this record's own
# prose changes unreviewable — which is the opposite of what it was added for.
README.md text whitespace diff
expected-sources.tsv text whitespace diff
verify-sources.sh text whitespace diff
.gitattributes text whitespace diff
